      "def:normalized-risk"
    ],
    "justification": "This identity exposes weight mismatch and chronological allocation regret as the only two sources of design loss.",
    "gap": "Li and Simchi-Levi decompose feasible-estimator error, and Dai, Gradu, and Harshaw connect Neyman regret to variance, but neither identity retains exact-count batch grids and arbitrary deterministic batch weights.",
    "consumer": "It turns an update-cadence choice in the Amazon adaptive-abtester workflow into two auditable cost components.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(d\\in\\mathfrak D_{n,B}\\).  Conditional on \\(\\mathcal F_{b-1}\\) and the batch-\n\\(b\\) assignment vector, the revealed responses are independent Gaussian variables with their arm-specific means and variances.  The exact-count bounds make both cells nonempty, and hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(D_b\\mid\\mathcal F_{b-1},Z_{I_b})=\\mu_1-\\mu_0=\\tau,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Var}(D_b\\mid\\mathcal F_{b-1},Z_{I_b})\n=\\frac{\\sigma_1^2}{Q_b}+\\frac{\\sigma_0^2}{m_b-Q_b}\n=\\frac{f_\\sigma(p_b)}{m_b}.\n\\]\nThe right side depends on the assignment vector only through its predictable exact count, so the same two identities hold after conditioning only on \\(\\mathcal F_{b-1}\\).  Thus each \\(D_b\\) is unbiased for \\(\\tau\\).  If \\(a<b\\), then \\(D_a-\\tau\\) is \\(\\mathcal F_{b-1}\\)-measurable and conditional centering gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(D_a-\\tau)(D_b-\\tau)]\n=\\mathbb E\\!\\left[(D_a-\\tau)\\mathbb E(D_b-\\tau\\mid\\mathcal F_{b-1})\\right]=0.\n\\]\nAssumption ass:deterministic-simplex-weights now yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(\\widehat\\tau_d)=\\sum_{b=1}^Bw_b\\tau=\\tau\n\\]\nand, using \\(m_b=nt_b\\),\n\\[\nn\\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat\\tau_d)\n=\\sum_{b=1}^B\\frac{w_b^2}{t_b}\\,\\mathbb E[f_\\sigma(p_b)]\n=V^*(\\sigma)\\sum_{b=1}^B\\frac{w_b^2}{t_b}\n+\\; +\\;\\sum_{b=1}^B\\frac{w_b^2}{t_b}\\,\\mathbb E[r_\\sigma(p_b)].\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\sum_bw_b=\\sum_bt_b=1\\), direct expansion gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^B\\frac{(w_b-t_b)^2}{t_b}\n=\\sum_{b=1}^B\\frac{w_b^2}{t_b}-2\\sum_{b=1}^Bw_b+\\sum_{b=1}^Bt_b\n=\\sum_{b=1}^B\\frac{w_b^2}{t_b}-1.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(V^*(\\sigma)\\) according to def:normalized-risk proves the asserted identity.  The expectation throughout is under \\(P^d_{\\mu,\\sigma}\\), whose existence was established in prop:observed-law-wellposed.",
